(area "memory" :when "the agent is about to remember, recall, revise or forget something, or a step just failed and the fix is now known")

(memory "memory/triggers" :when "the agent is about to remember something that should come back on its own when a situation, a command, an error, a result or certain words show up" :links ("memory/call-triggers"))
Two ways to fire by itself.

:when "situation" is the usual one: one string describing the situation that
needs the memory. Recognized by meaning before every step, not regex, against
whole conversation, latest step weighed most: what user said, your last code,
its result or its error. Recognizer also reads memory key and start of body.
Fires once per user message. Describe the whole situation, not only a request:
what user asks, what you are about to do, an error or a result that just came back.

  :when "agent is about to drive a web page, or a page call failed with a missing browser"

:unless "situation" beside :when names look-alike that must NOT fire it. Add
one when memory fired where it did not help:

  :when "agent is about to drive a web page" :unless "agent only reads a page's text with fetch"

:on is a quoted form of kind and pattern, for an exact event. Eight kinds:

  call     a form about to be evaluated, at any depth, so it catches a call
           nested inside a setq or a let
  result   the printed value a top-level form returned
  error    an evaluation that raised, so a past mistake surfaces exactly as it
           is about to repeat
  step     every step
  prose    words in the prose around your forms
  user     words in what the user just said
  recall   another memory being retrieved
  start    the user's first message, once, with no pattern: written (start)

One trigger per memory: :when or :on, not both. Memory with neither never
arrives by itself. It waits for a recall naming it. Unguessable key later
means lost.

(memory "memory/call-triggers" :when "the agent is writing a memory that must fire on one particular call, such as one tool or one server, and not on every call")
For call the pattern is A FORM, written like the call, matched structurally:

(call (load-mcp "acme")) only that server, not every load
(call (load-mcp)) any call to load-mcp, whatever its arguments
(call (load-mcp _)) _ matches one argument, whatever it is
(call (acme/.*)) a head is a regular expression, so one
                                   pattern hooks every tool on a server
(call (setq _ (acme/get _))) the shape nests as deeply as you write it

Pattern list matches a PREFIX of the call: fewer arguments looser, more
tighter. Every other kind matches text: pattern is a string regex, matched
case-insensitively anywhere:

(user "file.*") whatever the user says about file.md, file.pdf or any other one
(user "^deploy") only what the user opens with the word

Invalid regex is read as plain text, so a literal with a stray bracket still
matches.

Combine with all, any-of and not, in either sort of trigger:

(call (all (load-mcp) (not (load-mcp "acme"))))
(error (any-of "undefined" "void variable"))

Memory on a loader call surfaces what you know about a server the moment you
load it. Memory on an error surfaces the fix the moment that error returns.

NAME THE ARGUMENT THAT MADE IT TRUE. Facts about one server are not true of
the next, so (call (load-mcp "acme")) is right where (call (load-mcp)) would
fire on every server and teach wrong things about most. Hook the whole call,
not only the name.

Triggers watch code you write into the REPL. A form evaluated inside a replay or a function body is not a
step and fires nothing.

(memory "memory/fading" :when "the agent cannot find something it remembered, or the user asks why it forgot or how long a memory lasts")
STRENGTH AND FORGETTING

Every memory has a score. Recall strengthens it. Unused, it fades, half its
strength a week, and below a floor it is swept away. No hand tidying needed.
memory/forget exists for what is wrong, not for what is merely old.

Built-in memories never fade.

(memory "memory/revising" :when "a memory that just fired or was recalled turned out wrong or vague, or the user says the agent got something wrong and wants it fixed for next time")
THE WINDOW

Memory recalled or fired is open: until step end you can revise its body.
Outside that window revise errors, telling you to recall first. Fix a memory
while looking at it:

(memory/recall "deploy/notes")
(memory/revise "deploy/notes" "deploys go through task deploy, not kubectl")

Built-in wrong? Remember the correction under a new key, with a trigger that
will repeat.

(memory "memory/bodies" :when "the agent is about to save a recipe, a function or a reusable procedure, or remember just refused a live value")
GOTCHAS

- Body must be prose or a readable form. Closure, promise or secret is a live
  value, prints in angle brackets, and remember rejects it. Store the quoted
  form that builds it, never the built thing.
- A recipe worth keeping belongs in a memory, not a defun. Memories outlive the
  interpreter; definitions die on reset.
- Replaying a defun installs that function. Remembered recipe becomes running
  one.

(memory "memory/writing" :when "the agent is about to save, change or forget a memory, or the user asks what built-in memories exist")
Memory = key + body. Body is prose or code, one slot: here they are the same material.

- Prose body is something to know. Quoted form body is a recipe to run later.
  Quote it: arguments are evaluated, so unquoted form stores its result instead.
- (memory/replay key) evaluate a memory whose body is a form, return result.
- (memory/forget-all) drop every memory, only when the user asks for a clean slate.
- Names are slashed: bare remember, recall, forget are English verbs and would
  be read as code in an aside.

BUILT-IN MEMORIES: tool ships its own memories: how-to depth behind each extension,
loaded when its trigger fires. Found by memory/recall like yours. Read-only:
remember, revise, forget on a built-in key error.
- (memory/areas) gives (area count) per extension area, e.g. ("mcp" 7) .
- (memory/builtins "mcp") gives (key situation) for every built-in in that
  area; (memory/builtins) lists all. Recall a key to read its body.

(memory "memory/rules" :when "a guess just failed and the fix is now known, or the agent is deciding what is worth remembering")
- GIVE IT THE TRIGGER THAT WILL REPEAT, not a topic description: memory/hooks.
- Remember the correction, not the transcript. "browser_navigate, not navigate"
  is worth keeping; the whole tool listing is not.
- Do not remember what the REPL can tell you: doc output, discovery results,
  anything recomputable.

(memory "memory/recalling" :when "the agent is about to guess a name, a fact or a procedure it may have learned before, or must see which built-in memories an extension ships, or the user asks what it remembers")
- (memory/recall query) retrieves by key or body text, strongest first. Query
  is words, not a pattern: every meaningful word must land in the one memory.
  Spelling forgiven, prefix counts. Found memories load into your context:
  recalling IS reading. No echo after it.
- (memories) every memory as key, score and trigger, strongest first.
- Built-ins by area: memory/writing.
- (memory/remember key body) stores one.
- (memory/forget key) drops one for good. (memory/revise key body) corrects
  one inside its window: memory/revising.

(memory "memory/learning" :when "a step just failed and the agent worked out what works, or it found a solution worth keeping for later")
Memory is for what you found out the hard way. Step failed, you worked out what works: rejected call, wrong name, backwards argument order. Store the fix with `memory/remember` before moving on, while the right answer is on screen. That moment is the only one you get. The solution is either kind of memory. A DECLARATIVE one is prose, what you now know: "the tool is acme/get_widget, not acme/get". A PROCEDURAL one is a quoted form, the thing that worked, a call or a `defun` you replay later: `(memory/remember "widget-id" '(defun widget-id (w) (cdr (assoc "id" w)))) `. Same slot, both kinds.
SPEND THE REVISE WINDOW. Memory arrived vague, half-right, or missing the argument that mattered? Sharpen it with `(memory/revise key body) ` on the spot, while you see both what it said and what was true. Recall, use, revise: each use leaves the memory more useful than it was.

(memory "memory/hooks" :when "the agent is about to give a memory a trigger, :when or :on, so a lesson fires before the mistake")
HOOK IT TO WHAT PRECEDES THE ERROR, and NEVER TO THE CALL THAT FAILED. Hooking the failing form is the one trigger guaranteed to be useless: `:on '(call (acme/get _)) ` on a name that does not exist fires only while you are making the mistake a second time, too late to stop it, and if the next wrong guess is spelled differently it never fires at all. Before you write a trigger, check it against the step you just lost: would this have reached me BEFORE I wrote the bad form? If it only fires as the mistake happens, it is the wrong trigger.
HOOK THE LAST THING THAT WORKED ON THE WAY IN, the gate you always pass through before the moment of choice. After loading a server you name its tools, so what you learn about those names belongs on the load: `:on '(call (load-mcp "acme")) `, which puts the correction in front of you while the server is still coming up and before you can name a single tool. Ask what you will be doing just before you need this again, and hook THAT. Keep `:on '(error "...") ` for a failure with no such gate, where the error text is the earliest warning that exists. A memory you have to remember to look for will not be there when you need it.
A `:when` describes that moment in words: `:when "the agent is about to name a tool of the acme server"`, never `:when "acme/get failed"`.
